pg:CO1 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Take a URL apart and build one with urllib.parse, open pages with webbrowser, and read a robots.txt before fetching"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can take a URL apart and build one with urllib.parse, open pages with webbrowser, and read a robots.txt before fetching, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter13.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  ; sen0414:servesOutcome <http://example.org/sen0414#LO7> .
pg:CO2 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Download a page or a file with requests, check the status code, raise an error for a failed request and save the body in binary chunks, and tell which of these were read and not run here"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can download a page or a file with requests, check the status code, raise an error for a failed request and save the body in binary chunks, and tell which of these were read and not run here, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter13.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  ; sen0414:servesOutcome <http://example.org/sen0414#LO7> .
pg:CO3 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Find elements in HTML by a CSS selector with Beautiful Soup, read their text and attributes, and explain why a regular expression is the wrong tool for HTML"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can find elements in HTML by a CSS selector with Beautiful Soup, read their text and attributes, and explain why a regular expression is the wrong tool for HTML, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter13.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  ; sen0414:servesOutcome <http://example.org/sen0414#LO7> .
pg:CO4 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Decode the JSON answer of a web API, recognise its error answers, and convert its values"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can decode the JSON answer of a web API, recognise its error answers, and convert its values, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter13.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  ; sen0414:servesOutcome <http://example.org/sen0414#LO7> .
pg:CO5 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Analyze which tool a page needs - a downloader with a parser, or a browser driver such as Selenium or Playwright - and which claims of the chapter differ in current libraries and sites"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can analyze which tool a page needs - a downloader with a parser, or a browser driver such as Selenium or Playwright - and which claims of the chapter differ in current libraries and sites, at the analyze level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter13.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Analyze  ; sen0414:servesOutcome <http://example.org/sen0414#LO7> .
pg:Q1 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "What does urlsplit('https://site.example/books/find?title=py&page=2#top').query give?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q1_O0, pg:Q1_O1, pg:Q1_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q1_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO1 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The part after the number sign is the fragment, a separate part of the address." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "That is the path followed by the query; the query starts after the question mark." .
pg:Q1_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'title=py&page=2'" .
pg:Q1_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'title=py&page=2#top'" .
pg:Q1_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'/books/find?title=py&page=2'" .
pg:Q2 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "urllib.robotparser reads ['User-agent: *', 'Disallow: /private/']. What does can_fetch('*', 'https://site.example/private/c') return, and what does it mean for the server?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q2_O0, pg:Q2_O1, pg:Q2_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q2_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO1 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "RFC 9309 says the rules are not a form of access authorization; the server may still answer." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "can_fetch answers whether the rules allow that agent to fetch that address, and these rules do not." .
pg:Q2_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "False - a request to crawlers to stay out, not an access control" .
pg:Q2_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "False - the server will refuse the request with status 403" .
pg:Q2_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "True - can_fetch only reports whether the site is online" .
pg:Q3 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "The chapter downloads a file of 178,978 bytes with iter_content(100000) and writes each chunk. Which sizes are written?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q3_O0, pg:Q3_O1, pg:Q3_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q3_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Two chunks hold 178,978 bytes: one full chunk and the remainder." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "iter_content hands the body over in pieces of the size given, so the file is not written in one piece." .
pg:Q3_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "100000 and 78978" .
pg:Q3_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "100000, 100000 and 78978" .
pg:Q3_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "178978 in one piece" .
pg:Q4 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Why does the chapter call raise_for_status() right after requests.get()?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q4_O0, pg:Q4_O1, pg:Q4_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q4_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Understand ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "get() returns a Response for a 404 as well; only a connection failure or a timeout raises on its own." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Saving is done by write() on a file opened in 'wb' mode." .
pg:Q4_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "A failed download such as 404 is still a Response with text; the call turns it into an exception" .
pg:Q4_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "get() already raises an exception for every status code except 200" .
pg:Q4_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It saves the response body to a file" .
pg:Q5 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A page is saved with open(name, 'wb') and f.write('café'.encode('utf-8')). What does write() return?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q5_O0, pg:Q5_O1, pg:Q5_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q5_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The é takes two bytes in UTF-8, so four characters are five bytes." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "write() returns a count, not the text." .
pg:Q5_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "5, the number of bytes written" .
pg:Q5_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "4, the number of characters" .
pg:Q5_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'café'" .
pg:Q6 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "On the chapter's example page, soup.select('#author') is called. What comes back?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q6_O0, pg:Q6_O1, pg:Q6_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q6_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO3 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "select() always returns a list of the matching elements, even when there is one." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "get_text() on a Tag gives the text; select() itself returns the Tags." .
pg:Q6_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "A list with one Tag, whose get_text() is 'Al Sweigart'" .
pg:Q6_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "One Tag, not a list" .
pg:Q6_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "The text 'Al Sweigart' as a string" .
pg:Q7 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A regular expression written for <a href='...'> finds no link in <a href='https://a.example/y'> and none in <a class='n' href='https://a.example/z'>. What does this show?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q7_O0, pg:Q7_O1, pg:Q7_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q7_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Understand ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO3 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Each fix handles one more variant and leaves others; the chapter advises a parser for that reason." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "A parser reads both quoting styles and the extra attribute, and returns all three addresses." .
pg:Q7_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "HTML can be written in many valid ways, which a parser handles and a pattern does not" .
pg:Q7_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "The regular expression has a typing mistake and a corrected pattern will do" .
pg:Q7_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Beautiful Soup cannot read single quotes either" .
pg:Q8 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "285.44 Kelvin comes back from OpenWeather. Which expression gives Celsius?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q8_O0, pg:Q8_O1, pg:Q8_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q8_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO4 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "That expression converts to Fahrenheit." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Adding moves away from Celsius; zero Celsius is 273.15 Kelvin." .
pg:Q8_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "round(285.44 - 273.15, 1), which is 12.3" .
pg:Q8_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "round(285.44 * (9 / 5) - 459.67, 1), which is 54.1" .
pg:Q8_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "round(285.44 + 273.15, 1)" .
pg:Q9 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A call with a wrong latitude is answered with {'cod': '400', 'message': 'wrong latitude'}. What does response_data[0] do on it?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q9_O0, pg:Q9_O1, pg:Q9_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q9_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO4 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Index 0 is looked up as a key; the message is under the key 'message'." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "An empty list is the answer for an unknown city, a different shape." .
pg:Q9_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Raises KeyError, because the answer is a dictionary and not a list" .
pg:Q9_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Gives 'wrong latitude'" .
pg:Q9_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Gives an empty list" .
pg:Q10 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A page's only paragraph is written by JavaScript after the page loads. What do requests and Beautiful Soup find, and what finds the text?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q10_O0, pg:Q10_O1, pg:Q10_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q10_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO5 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "requests downloads the HTML as the server sent it and runs no script." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "requests downloads the script text without complaint; the element is simply empty." .
pg:Q10_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "An empty element; a driven browser such as Selenium or Playwright" .
pg:Q10_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "The text, because requests runs the script" .
pg:Q10_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "An error, because requests refuses such pages" .
pg:Q11 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "On 2026-10-12 the chapter's searchpypi.py was run unchanged against pypi.org. requests got status 200, a page titled Client Challenge and no .package-snippet element. What did the program do?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q11_O0, pg:Q11_O1, pg:Q11_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q11_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO5 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The status was 200, so raise_for_status() has nothing to raise; the page is simply not the results page." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The tabs are opened from the links found, and there were none." .
pg:Q11_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Opened no tab: min(5, 0) is 0" .
pg:Q11_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Raised HTTPError, because the status was not 200" .
pg:Q11_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Opened five tabs of the challenge page" .
pg:Q12 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "The rules ['User-agent: *', 'Disallow: /search*'] are read by urllib.robotparser. Can '*' fetch https://pypi.org/search/?q=boring+stuff?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q12_O0, pg:Q12_O1, pg:Q12_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q12_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO5 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Before 3.14.5 the star in a path was taken literally, so the search page was allowed." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "From 3.14.5 the module follows RFC 9309 and reads the star as a wildcard." .
pg:Q12_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Yes under Python 3.14.0 to 3.14.4, no from 3.14.5" .
pg:Q12_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "No under every Python 3.14" .
pg:Q12_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Yes under every Python 3.14" .
pg:Protocol a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Protocol"@en ;
    skos:definition "Chapter 13 starts from a plain fact: every tool in it, from a one-line webbrowser call to a driven Firefox, ends up sending a request to a web address and reading what comes back, and the chapter's first section, HTTP and HTTPS, explains the address and the protocol (Sweigart, 2025). A program that does this is a guest on someone else's computer, and that adds questions of manners that a reader of the book does not meet until a site says no."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_Protocol> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Protocol" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Protocol .
pg:AddressForms a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Address forms"@en ;
    skos:definition "The web address is the first thing every program of the chapter needs, and the chapter reads two of them closely: the OpenStreetMap search address of Project 6 and the OpenWeather endpoint of the weather section (Sweigart, 2025). Both are URLs, a string with a fixed grammar that a program can take apart and put together without guessing."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_AddressForms> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AddressForms" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Protocol .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AddressForms .
pg:UrlAnatomy a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Url anatomy"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter breaks https://api.openweathermap.org/geo/1.0/direct?q={city_name},{state_code},{country_code}&appid={API_key} into the scheme https://, the domain name api.openweathermap.org, the path /geo/1.0/direct and the query string that follows the question mark (Sweigart, 2025). RFC 3986 gives the general grammar behind it: a scheme, an authority, a path, an optional query and an optional fragment (Internet Engineering Task Force, 2005)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_UrlAnatomy> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-UrlAnatomy" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:AddressForms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:UrlAnatomy .
pg:QueryString a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Query string"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter explains that in URL encoding the parameter name and the argument value are separated by an equal sign and that several pairs are separated by an ampersand, and it fills the pairs from variables with an f-string (Sweigart, 2025). The query string is how a program tells a service what it wants: which city, which key."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_QueryString> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-QueryString" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:AddressForms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:QueryString .
pg:PercentEncoding a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Percent encoding"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter notes that the browser handles necessary URL encoding by itself, such as turning a space into %20 (Sweigart, 2025). Percent-encoding is the rule behind that: a character that a URL may not carry is written as a percent sign and the two hexadecimal digits of its byte, so that a space becomes %20 and a comma %2C."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_PercentEncoding> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PercentEncoding" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:AddressForms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PercentEncoding .
pg:FragmentPart a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Fragment part"@en ;
    skos:definition "The OpenStreetMap address that the chapter copies from the address bar ends in #map=19/37.75897/-122.42142, and the chapter tests that the part after the number sign can be removed and the page still loads (Sweigart, 2025). That part is the fragment: it names a place inside the page, here the zoom level and the map centre, and the browser uses it, the server does not receive it."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_FragmentPart> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FragmentPart" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:AddressForms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FragmentPart .
pg:ProtocolBasics a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Protocol basics"@en ;
    skos:definition "Once the address is understood, the chapter says what the program talks to the server in: HTTP, in its encrypted form HTTPS, and a server answers every request with a status code (Sweigart, 2025). Every later check of the chapter, from raise_for_status to the empty list of a failed search, rests on these answers."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_ProtocolBasics> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ProtocolBasics" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Protocol .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ProtocolBasics .
pg:HttpsEncryption a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Https encryption"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter explains that HTTPS is an encrypted version of HTTP, so that identity thieves, national intelligence agencies or an internet service provider cannot view the pages visited or the passwords and card numbers submitted; a VPN only moves that visibility to the VPN provider, and the identity of the website visited can still be known (Sweigart, 2025). Most sites now encrypt all traffic, not just the pages with a password."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_HttpsEncryption> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-HttpsEncryption" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ProtocolBasics .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:HttpsEncryption .
pg:StatusCode a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Status code"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter names 200 as the code for OK, 404 as Not Found, and compares status_code with requests.codes.ok (Sweigart, 2025). A status code is the first line of every answer: three digits that tell the program whether the request worked before it reads any content."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_StatusCode> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-StatusCode" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ProtocolBasics .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:StatusCode .
pg:UserAgent a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "User agent"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that the user-agent string is a major tell that a script is visiting, that the string of the requests module is something like python-requests/X.XX.X, and that a site such as whatsmyua.info shows your own (Sweigart, 2025). MDN describes the User-Agent request header as a characteristic string that lets servers identify the application, operating system, vendor and version of the requesting user agent (MDN Web Docs, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_UserAgent> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-UserAgent" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ProtocolBasics .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:UserAgent .
pg:CrawlerManners a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Crawler manners"@en ;
    skos:definition "A program that fetches many pages is a guest, and the chapter's only rules of conduct are in its comic downloader: limit the number of downloads so as not to eat the bandwidth of the site, and pause after each page (Sweigart, 2025). The chapter as read does not mention robots.txt, the file in which a site states its own rules."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_CrawlerManners> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CrawlerManners" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Protocol .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CrawlerManners .
pg:RobotsTxt a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Robots txt"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter as read never mentions robots.txt. RFC 9309 defines it as a plain-text file at the root of a site whose rules crawlers are requested to honour, and states that these rules are not a form of access authorization (Internet Engineering Task Force, 2022)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_RobotsTxt> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RobotsTxt" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CrawlerManners .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RobotsTxt .
pg:PoliteDelay a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Polite delay"@en ;
    skos:definition "The comic downloader limits itself to ten downloads by default so as not to eat up the bandwidth of the site, and after every comic it calls time.sleep(1) to avoid hammering the web server, which the chapter defines as impolitely downloading as fast as possible (Sweigart, 2025). The pause is a courtesy that costs the reader one second per page."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_PoliteDelay> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PoliteDelay" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CrawlerManners .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PoliteDelay .
pg:Fetching a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Fetching"@en ;
    skos:definition "The first tools of the chapter fetch a page in two different ways. The webbrowser module hands an address to the browser the user already has, so the user sees the page, and the requests module downloads the page into the program as text or bytes, so the program sees it (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_Fetching> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Fetching" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Fetching .
pg:BrowserOpening a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Browser opening"@en ;
    skos:definition "Project 6 turns a few seconds of copying and pasting into one command: showmap.py reads an address from the command line or from the clipboard and opens the OpenStreetMap page for it (Sweigart, 2025). Everything it needs is a URL and the one function of the webbrowser module."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_BrowserOpening> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BrowserOpening" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Fetching .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BrowserOpening .
pg:WebbrowserOpen a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Webbrowser open"@en ;
    skos:definition "webbrowser.open('https://inventwithpython.com/') opens a browser tab at that address, and the chapter calls this about the only thing the module can do (Sweigart, 2025). The module launches the user's own browser and then lets go: the program cannot read the page that opened."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_WebbrowserOpen> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WebbrowserOpen" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BrowserOpening .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WebbrowserOpen .
pg:CommandLineArguments a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Command line arguments"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter reads the words typed after the program name from sys.argv, a list of strings whose first item is the script name, and joins sys.argv[1:] with spaces to rebuild the address '777 Valencia St, San Francisco, CA 94110' (Sweigart, 2025). If the list has only the script name, len(sys.argv) is 1 and the program falls back to the clipboard."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_CommandLineArguments> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CommandLineArguments" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BrowserOpening .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CommandLineArguments .
pg:ShowmapProgram a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Showmap program"@en ;
    skos:definition "showmap.py joins the arguments into an address or, when there are none, reads pyperclip.paste(), and calls webbrowser.open('https://www.openstreetmap.org/search?query=' + address); the chapter says the browser handles encoding such as a space turned into %20 (Sweigart, 2025). Table 13-1 compares its three steps with the seven steps of doing it by hand."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_ShowmapProgram> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ShowmapProgram" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BrowserOpening .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ShowmapProgram .
pg:LocalHelpFile a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Local help file"@en ;
    skos:definition "Among the ideas for similar programs the chapter lists opening a local .html file as a help page, with the file:// prefix instead of https://, for example file:///C:/Users/al/Desktop/help.html on Windows or file:///Users/al/Desktop/help.html on macOS (Sweigart, 2025). A page can have fonts, colour, tables and images that print() cannot give."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_LocalHelpFile> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LocalHelpFile" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BrowserOpening .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LocalHelpFile .
pg:PageDownload a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Page download"@en ;
    skos:definition "requests.get is the chapter's tool for taking a page into the program rather than showing it to the user (Sweigart, 2025). It handles network errors, routing and compression, and what it returns is a Response object that holds the answer to the request."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_PageDownload> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PageDownload" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Fetching .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PageDownload .
pg:GetRequest a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Get request"@en ;
    skos:definition "requests.get() takes a string URL and returns a Response object, and type() prints it as <class 'requests.models.Response'>; the module is not part of Python and must be installed before use (Sweigart, 2025). In the chapter it fetches the whole of Romeo and Juliet in one line."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_GetRequest> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-GetRequest" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PageDownload .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:GetRequest .
pg:ResponseBody a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Response body"@en ;
    skos:definition "The downloaded page is stored as a string in the Response's text attribute, and the chapter reminds the reader that a download with requests brings only the HTML, not the images and other media, which must be fetched separately (Sweigart, 2025). Its example is rj.txt, the whole of Romeo and Juliet."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_ResponseBody> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ResponseBody" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PageDownload .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ResponseBody .
pg:RequestTimeout a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Request timeout"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter as read never mentions a timeout. The requests documentation says that a timeout in seconds tells it to stop waiting, and that nearly all production code should use the parameter in nearly all requests, because without it a program can hang indefinitely (Requests documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_RequestTimeout> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RequestTimeout" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PageDownload .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RequestTimeout .
pg:ResponseChecking a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Response checking"@en ;
    skos:definition "A download can fail in two ways, and the chapter gives two checks: compare status_code with requests.codes.ok, or call raise_for_status and let an exception stop the program (Sweigart, 2025). It adds that a program should generally stop at once when something unexpected happens."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_ResponseChecking> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ResponseChecking" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Fetching .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ResponseChecking .
pg:StatusCheck a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Status check"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter checks a download by comparing the Response's status_code with requests.codes.ok, whose value is 200 (Sweigart, 2025). The test is the first thing the program does with an answer."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_StatusCheck> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-StatusCheck" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ResponseChecking .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:StatusCheck .
pg:RaiseForStatus a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Raise for status"@en ;
    skos:definition "raise_for_status() does nothing if the download worked and raises an exception if it failed, so the chapter calls it right after every get(), or wraps it in try and except and prints f'There was a problem: {exc}' (Sweigart, 2025). Its listing shows 404 Client Error: Not Found for a page that does not exist."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_RaiseForStatus> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RaiseForStatus" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ResponseChecking .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RaiseForStatus .
pg:FileSaving a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "File saving"@en ;
    skos:definition "Saving what was downloaded is the third step of the chapter's review of the process: open a file in write binary mode, loop over iter_content and write each chunk (Sweigart, 2025). Two details make it work, the mode and the loop."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_FileSaving> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FileSaving" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Fetching .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FileSaving .
pg:BinaryWrite a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Binary write"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter writes the page to a file opened with 'wb', because even a page in plaintext must be written as binary data to keep the Unicode encoding of the text, and write() returns the number of bytes written (Sweigart, 2025). A text-mode file would translate the text itself."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_BinaryWrite> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BinaryWrite" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FileSaving .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BinaryWrite .
pg:ChunkedDownload a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Chunked download"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter loops over response.iter_content(100000) and writes each chunk, and the two numbers it prints, 100000 and 78978, are the bytes written for each chunk of a file of 178,978 bytes; each chunk is of the bytes data type (Sweigart, 2025). The size asked for is the most a chunk can hold, so the last one is the remainder."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_ChunkedDownload> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ChunkedDownload" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FileSaving .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ChunkedDownload .
pg:Services a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Services"@en ;
    skos:definition "Some of the data a program wants is not on a page made for eyes but offered by a service made for programs, and the chapter's example is the weather: a program asks OpenWeather for the coordinates of a city and then for its weather, and gets JSON back (Sweigart, 2025). The service's API, its application programming interface, is the specification that says how to ask."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_Services> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Services" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Services .
pg:ApiCalls a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Api calls"@en ;
    skos:definition "A call to a web API is a URL with a path, a query and a key, and the program that makes it is a client of the service (Sweigart, 2025). The chapter makes two calls in a row, the coordinates of San Francisco and then its current weather, and the second needs the answer of the first."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_ApiCalls> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ApiCalls" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Services .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ApiCalls .
pg:ServiceEndpoint a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Service endpoint"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter calls the specific URL used for one API request the endpoint, builds two of them for OpenWeather, /geo/1.0/direct for the latitude and longitude of a city and /data/2.5/weather for its current weather, and notes that services put a version number in the URL and may later deprecate old versions (Sweigart, 2025). The geocoding documentation shows the same endpoint with an optional limit parameter for cities that share a name (OpenWeather documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_ServiceEndpoint> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ServiceEndpoint" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ApiCalls .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ServiceEndpoint .
pg:AccessKey a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Access key"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that the API key is sort of a password that identifies the account in API requests, that it must be kept secret, and that a program should read it from a text file instead of including it in the source, so that the program can be shared; it warns that the key in its example is fake (Sweigart, 2025). Almost every service asks for a registered email address in return."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_AccessKey> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AccessKey" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ApiCalls .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AccessKey .
pg:ApiData a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Api data"@en ;
    skos:definition "What a service returns is text, most often JSON, and the program decodes it into Python lists and dictionaries and then converts the values it needs (Sweigart, 2025). Four details of the chapter's weather answers repay a close look."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_ApiData> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ApiData" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Services .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ApiData .
pg:ErrorAnswer a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Error answer"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that an unknown city gives an empty list and that a wrong latitude or longitude gives a dictionary such as {'cod': '400', 'message': 'wrong latitude'} (Sweigart, 2025). A failed call is therefore still answered, with a different shape of data."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_ErrorAnswer> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ErrorAnswer" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ApiData .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ErrorAnswer .
pg:JsonDecoding a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Json decoding"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that json.loads(response.text) returns a Python data structure of lists and dictionaries, so that response_data[0]['lat'] is a float, 37.7790262, and response_data[0]['lon'] is -122.419906 (Sweigart, 2025). JSON is the text format that RFC 8259 defines for exchanging structured data (Internet Engineering Task Force, 2017)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_JsonDecoding> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-JsonDecoding" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ApiData .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:JsonDecoding .
pg:UnitConversion a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Unit conversion"@en ;
    skos:definition "OpenWeather returns the temperature in Kelvin, and the chapter converts the 285.44 it prints with round(285.44 - 273.15, 1), which is 12.3 Celsius, and round(285.44 * (9 / 5) - 459.67, 1), which is 54.1 Fahrenheit (Sweigart, 2025). The conversion is the program's job, since the service chooses the unit."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_UnitConversion> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-UnitConversion" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ApiData .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:UnitConversion .
pg:ForecastTimestamps a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Forecast timestamps"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that response_data['list'] holds 40 dictionaries of forecasts at three-hour increments for the next five days, that each dt is a Unix epoch timestamp, and that datetime.datetime.fromtimestamp() turns it into a datetime object (Sweigart, 2025). Forty entries three hours apart are exactly five days."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_ForecastTimestamps> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ForecastTimestamps" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ApiData .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ForecastTimestamps .
pg:OtherServices a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Other services"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter ends the weather section by naming other free weather APIs, weather.gov and weatherapi.com, and saying that a third-party Python package on PyPI may already wrap an API so that functions handle the endpoints and the parsing (Sweigart, 2025). Every API works differently, though they are often reached over HTTPS with requests and answer in JSON or XML."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_OtherServices> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-OtherServices" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Services .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:OtherServices .
pg:WeatherServices a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Weather services"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter names weather.gov as a site with its own free weather API (Sweigart, 2025). The National Weather Service documents its API as open data free to use, with rate limits that are not public, and requires a User-Agent that identifies the application, with contact information if possible (National Weather Service, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_WeatherServices> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WeatherServices" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:OtherServices .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WeatherServices .
pg:Parsing a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Parsing"@en ;
    skos:definition "A page downloaded with requests is one long string of HTML, and the data wanted is somewhere inside it. The chapter's answer is to learn just enough HTML to read a page, to look at the page the way the browser's tools show it, and to hand the string to a parser, Beautiful Soup, instead of searching it by hand (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_Parsing> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Parsing" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Parsing .
pg:MarkupBasics a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Markup basics"@en ;
    skos:definition "An HTML file is a plaintext file with the .html extension whose text is surrounded by tags, words in angle brackets; a starting tag, its content and a closing tag form an element, and a tag can carry attributes (Sweigart, 2025). The chapter assumes some HTML and gives pointers for a beginner."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_MarkupBasics> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-MarkupBasics" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Parsing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:MarkupBasics .
pg:HtmlElement a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Html element"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter explains that the tags tell the browser how to format the page and that <b>Hello</b>, world! shows Hello in bold: the opening <b> tag starts the bold text, the closing </b> tag ends it, and together they form an element (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_HtmlElement> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-HtmlElement" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:MarkupBasics .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:HtmlElement .
pg:HtmlAttribute a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Html attribute"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter names href, the attribute of the a tag that says which URL a link goes to, and id, which identifies one element in the page and which the program seeks out, and the class attribute that searchpypi.py later uses as a marker (Sweigart, 2025). Finding the id with the Developer Tools is the common first task of a scraping program."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_HtmlAttribute> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-HtmlAttribute" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:MarkupBasics .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:HtmlAttribute .
pg:CharacterReferences a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Character references"@en ;
    skos:definition "In the file of example3.html the first paragraph reads This &lt;p&gt; tag puts content into a single paragraph, and the chapter prints the visible text, This <p> tag puts content into a single paragraph; the chapter's Selenium section explains that the &lt; and &gt; are HTML escape characters for the less-than and greater-than signs (Sweigart, 2025). A character reference is how a page writes a character that would otherwise start a tag."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_CharacterReferences> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CharacterReferences" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:MarkupBasics .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CharacterReferences .
pg:PageInspection a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Page inspection"@en ;
    skos:definition "Before writing a line of scraping code the chapter has the reader look: at the source the browser received, and at the live tree of the page in the Developer Tools (Sweigart, 2025). The programmer is doing research on the page, as a person does in a library before writing."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_PageInspection> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PageInspection" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Parsing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PageInspection .
pg:ViewSource a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "View source"@en ;
    skos:definition "The source of a page is the HTML the browser actually receives, from which it knows how to display, or render, the page; the chapter has the reader right-click and choose View page source, and tells them that requests brings only the HTML of the page (Sweigart, 2025). The reader is not expected to understand it all, only to pick data out."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_ViewSource> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ViewSource" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PageInspection .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ViewSource .
pg:DeveloperTools a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Developer tools"@en ;
    skos:definition "In Firefox, Chrome and Microsoft Edge, F12 opens and closes the Developer Tools, and right-clicking a part of the page and choosing Inspect Element shows the HTML responsible for it; the chapter finds, on the weather.gov forecast for the ZIP code 94105, a div element with the class col-sm-10 forecast-text, and warns that a redesign means repeating the process (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_DeveloperTools> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DeveloperTools" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PageInspection .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DeveloperTools .
pg:CssSelector a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Css selector"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Developer Tools can copy the CSS selector of an element, for instance div.row-odd:nth-child(1) > div:nth-child(2), and the chapter passes it to Beautiful Soup's select() or to Selenium's find_element(); it calls the full syntax and XPath beyond its scope and advises using a site's API when there is one, because the page changes more often (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_CssSelector> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CssSelector" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PageInspection .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CssSelector .
pg:ParsingAdvice a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Parsing advice"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's boxed warning is short and firm: do not use regular expressions to parse HTML (Sweigart, 2025). It is advice about method, given in the middle of the section on the Developer Tools, before any parser has been shown."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_ParsingAdvice> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ParsingAdvice" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Parsing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ParsingAdvice .
pg:RegexPitfall a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Regex pitfall"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter advises against regular expressions for HTML, XML, JSON, TOML or YAML because HTML can be formatted in many valid ways, and trying to capture all of them is tedious and error prone; a module made for parsing, such as bs4, is less likely to result in bugs (Sweigart, 2025). A pattern describes characters; a parser knows the grammar."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_RegexPitfall> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RegexPitfall" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ParsingAdvice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RegexPitfall .
pg:SoupObjects a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Soup objects"@en ;
    skos:definition "Beautiful Soup is a package for extracting information from an HTML page; the name to install is beautifulsoup4 and the name to import is bs4 (Sweigart, 2025). Its BeautifulSoup object holds the parsed page, and the select method returns the parts of it that match a CSS selector."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_SoupObjects> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SoupObjects" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Parsing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SoupObjects .
pg:SoupObject a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Soup object"@en ;
    skos:definition "bs4.BeautifulSoup() accepts a string containing HTML, or an open file, and a parser name, and returns a BeautifulSoup object; the chapter downloads https://autbor.com/example3.html with requests, parses res.text with 'html.parser' and then also loads the file from disk (Sweigart, 2025). html.parser tells Beautiful Soup to use the parser of Python's standard library."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_SoupObject> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SoupObject" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SoupObjects .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SoupObject .
pg:SelectMethod a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Select method"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter retrieves a page element by passing a CSS selector string to select(), which returns a list of Tag objects, one for every matching element (Sweigart, 2025). For '#author' it prints type ResultSet, length 1 and a Tag, and the sentence it uses is that select() gives a list of matches."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_SelectMethod> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SelectMethod" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SoupObjects .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SelectMethod .
pg:SelectorPatterns a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Selector patterns"@en ;
    skos:definition "Table 13-2 lists the common patterns: div, #author, .notice, div span, div > span, input[name] and input[type='button']; the chapter adds that they combine, so p #author matches an element with the id author that is inside a p element (Sweigart, 2025). A selector is a short address of an element in the page's tree."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_SelectorPatterns> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SelectorPatterns" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SoupObjects .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SelectorPatterns .
pg:TagText a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Tag text"@en ;
    skos:definition "Passing a Tag to str() shows its tags, and get_text() returns its text, the content between the opening and closing tags; for the span, str gives <span id='author'>Al Sweigart</span> and get_text gives 'Al Sweigart' (Sweigart, 2025). The chapter then does the same for the first three paragraphs."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_TagText> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TagText" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SoupObjects .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TagText .
pg:TagAttributes a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Tag attributes"@en ;
    skos:definition "The get() method of a Tag takes an attribute name and returns its value, and attrs is the dictionary of all attributes; the chapter shows get('id') giving 'author', a missing attribute giving None and attrs giving {'id': 'author'} (Sweigart, 2025). Tag text covers what lies between the tags, and this covers what lies inside them."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_TagAttributes> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TagAttributes" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SoupObjects .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TagAttributes .
pg:Programs a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Programs"@en ;
    skos:definition "The three projects and the three practice programs of the chapter put the tools together with a stop rule: take an address, fetch a page, find the next thing, and decide when to quit (Sweigart, 2025). Each is a short program, and each meets the real web, which changes and sometimes refuses."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_Programs> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Programs" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Programs .
pg:SearchProgram a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Search program"@en ;
    skos:definition "Project 7, searchpypi.py, takes search words from the command line, fetches the search page of the Python Package Index, finds the links of the results and opens the first five in browser tabs (Sweigart, 2025). The chapter says it can be adapted to other sites, though large ones make scraping their search pages difficult."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_SearchProgram> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SearchProgram" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Programs .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SearchProgram .
pg:SearchResultLinks a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Search result links"@en ;
    skos:definition "searchpypi.py fetches https://pypi.org/search/? followed by the arguments, makes a BeautifulSoup object, selects '.package-snippet' and opens min(5, len(link_elems)) tabs, adding https://pypi.org to each href; the chapter explains min() as the smaller of five and the number of links, so that fewer than five results do not crash the loop (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_SearchResultLinks> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SearchResultLinks" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SearchProgram .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SearchResultLinks .
pg:ChallengePage a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Challenge page"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter warns that Google, DuckDuckGo, Amazon and other large sites often employ measures that make scraping their search results pages difficult, and presents PyPI as a site where the program works (Sweigart, 2025). A measure of that kind is a page that is not the page asked for."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_ChallengePage> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ChallengePage" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SearchProgram .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ChallengePage .
pg:ComicProgram a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Comic program"@en ;
    skos:definition "Project 8, downloadXkcdComics.py, starts at the front page of a web comic, saves the image, follows the Previous link, and repeats until it reaches the first comic or the download limit (Sweigart, 2025). It is the chapter's example of a program that follows links to scrape large amounts of data."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_ComicProgram> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ComicProgram" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Programs .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ComicProgram .
pg:ComicCrawler a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Comic crawler"@en ;
    skos:definition "The program loops while not url.endswith('#') and num_downloads < MAX_DOWNLOADS: it downloads the page, selects '#comic img', downloads the image if one is found, and follows the href of 'a[rel='prev']'; the oldest comic's Prev button links to https://xkcd.com/#, which ends the loop, and the limit of ten stops it earlier (Sweigart, 2025). Some pages hold something other than an image, and the program skips them."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_ComicCrawler> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ComicCrawler" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ComicProgram .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ComicCrawler .
pg:ComicFileNames a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Comic file names"@en ;
    skos:definition "The comic_URL has a value like https://imgs.xkcd.com/comics/heartbleed_explanation.png, which looks like a file path, so os.path.basename() returns heartbleed_explanation.png and os.path.join('xkcd', ...) puts it in the xkcd folder with backslashes on Windows and forward slashes on macOS and Linux (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_ComicFileNames> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ComicFileNames" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ComicProgram .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ComicFileNames .
pg:IndentedListing a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Indented listing"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Step 4 listing of the chapter is indented with 8 spaces for image_file = open(...), 12 for the for loop, 15 for image_file.write, 12 for image_file.close and 6 for the lines that follow. (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_IndentedListing> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-IndentedListing" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ComicProgram .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:IndentedListing .
pg:PoliteCrawler a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Polite crawler"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter limits its downloads to ten and sleeps one second per page, and does not mention that a site publishes its own rules for crawlers (Sweigart, 2025). A polite crawler adds one step at the start: read robots.txt, skip what it disallows and wait what it asks."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_PoliteCrawler> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PoliteCrawler" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ComicProgram .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PoliteCrawler .
pg:PracticeWork a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Practice work"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter closes with a summary, 18 practice questions and three practice programs: an image site downloader, the game 2048 and link verification (Sweigart, 2025). The questions ask for definitions and short pieces of code; the programs ask for a complete script."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_PracticeWork> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PracticeWork" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Programs .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PracticeWork .
pg:LinkVerification a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Link verification"@en ;
    skos:definition "The practice program takes the URL of a page, finds every <a> link on it, tests whether the linked URL gives a 404 Not Found status and prints the broken links (Sweigart, 2025). It joins Status check, Soup object and Select method in one script."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_LinkVerification> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LinkVerification" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PracticeWork .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LinkVerification .
pg:ImageDownloader a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Image downloader"@en ;
    skos:definition "The first practice program goes to a photo-sharing site such as Flickr or Imgur, searches a category and downloads all the resulting images, in a form that works with any photo site that has a search feature (Sweigart, 2025). Large photo sites are the kind the chapter says make scraping difficult."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_ImageDownloader> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ImageDownloader" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PracticeWork .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ImageDownloader .
pg:GameKeystrokes a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Game keystrokes"@en ;
    skos:definition "The second practice program opens https://play2048.co and keeps sending up, right, down and left keystrokes to play 2048 automatically (Sweigart, 2025). The game runs in the page's own script, so requests and Beautiful Soup cannot play it."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_GameKeystrokes> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-GameKeystrokes" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PracticeWork .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:GameKeystrokes .
pg:PracticeQuestions a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Practice questions"@en ;
    skos:definition "Question 9 asks for the CSS selector string that would find the element with an id attribute of main, and question 10 the elements with an id attribute of highlight; the chapter's selector patterns make the answers #main and #highlight (Sweigart, 2025). The other sixteen questions ask for definitions that the sections above answer."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_PracticeQuestions> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PracticeQuestions" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PracticeWork .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PracticeQuestions .
pg:Browsers a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Browsers"@en ;
    skos:definition "Requests and Beautiful Soup work as long as the program can work out the URL of the page it wants and that page already contains the data. When the URL is hard to find, the page is built by a script, or the site wants a login, the chapter turns to a program that starts a real browser and drives it as a user does: first Selenium, then the newer Playwright (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_Browsers> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Browsers" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Browsers .
pg:SeleniumDriver a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Selenium driver"@en ;
    skos:definition "Selenium lets Python control the browser by programmatically clicking links and filling in forms as a human would, and because it launches a browser it is a bit slower and hard to run in the background (Sweigart, 2025). The chapter drives Firefox."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_SeleniumDriver> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SeleniumDriver" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Browsers .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SeleniumDriver .
pg:WebDriverStart a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Web driver start"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter imports Selenium with from selenium import webdriver, starts Firefox with webdriver.Firefox(), whose type prints as <class 'selenium.webdriver.firefox.webdriver.WebDriver'>, loads a page with browser.get(), and tells the reader to download geckodriver by hand if the error says it must be in PATH, or to use the webdriver-manager package (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_WebDriverStart> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WebDriverStart" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SeleniumDriver .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WebDriverStart .
pg:BrowserButtons a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Browser buttons"@en ;
    skos:definition "browser.back() clicks the Back button, forward() the Forward button, refresh() the Refresh button and quit() the Close Window button (Sweigart, 2025). They act on the browser itself and not on the page."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_BrowserButtons> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BrowserButtons" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SeleniumDriver .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BrowserButtons .
pg:SeleniumElements a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Selenium elements"@en ;
    skos:definition "The core of a Selenium program is finding an element on the page and doing something to it: reading its text, clicking it, typing into it (Sweigart, 2025). The chapter gives the finding methods in one table, the properties of the result in another, and then the actions."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_SeleniumElements> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SeleniumElements" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Browsers .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SeleniumElements .
pg:ByLocators a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "By locators"@en ;
    skos:definition "Table 13-3 lists By.CLASS_NAME, CSS_SELECTOR, ID, LINK_TEXT, PARTIAL_LINK_TEXT, NAME and TAG_NAME, each with the elements it returns, and notes that a tag name is matched without regard to case (Sweigart, 2025). The chapter passes them to find_element() and find_elements() as the first argument."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_ByLocators> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ByLocators" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SeleniumElements .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ByLocators .
pg:ElementLookup a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Element lookup"@en ;
    skos:definition "find_element() returns a single WebElement, the first match, and find_elements() returns a list of WebElements, one for every match; if nothing matches, Selenium raises an exception that the chapter calls NoSuchElement, which the program can catch with try and except (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_ElementLookup> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ElementLookup" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SeleniumElements .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ElementLookup .
pg:ElementProperties a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Element properties"@en ;
    skos:definition "Table 13-4 lists tag_name, get_attribute, get_property, text, clear, is_displayed, is_enabled, is_selected, location and size, and the chapter says that text is a shortcut for get_property('innerText'), with innerText and innerHTML as properties rather than attributes (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_ElementProperties> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ElementProperties" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SeleniumElements .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ElementProperties .
pg:ClickAndSubmit a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Click and submit"@en ;
    skos:definition "click() simulates a mouse click on an element, which follows a link, picks a radio button or presses a Submit button, and send_keys() types into an input or textarea; submit() on any element of a form acts as the Submit button of that form (Sweigart, 2025). The chapter fills the fake login of the example page and warns against putting passwords in source code."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_ClickAndSubmit> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ClickAndSubmit" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SeleniumElements .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ClickAndSubmit .
pg:SpecialKeys a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Special keys"@en ;
    skos:definition "Selenium represents keyboard keys in selenium.webdriver.common.keys, imported as Keys, and send_keys accepts Keys.ENTER, RETURN, HOME, END, PAGE_UP, PAGE_DOWN, UP, DOWN, LEFT, RIGHT, TAB, ESCAPE, BACK_SPACE, DELETE and F1 to F12; with the cursor in no text field, END and HOME scroll the page to its bottom and top (Sweigart, 2025). Sending them to the html element is the chapter's trick."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_SpecialKeys> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SpecialKeys" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SeleniumElements .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SpecialKeys .
pg:PlaywrightDriver a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Playwright driver"@en ;
    skos:definition "Playwright is a browser-controlling library similar to Selenium, but newer, with some features that merit learning, headless mode first among them, and its drivers are installed with one command (Sweigart, 2025). The chapter does not repeat the general scraping and selector material it gave for Selenium."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_PlaywrightDriver> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PlaywrightDriver" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Browsers .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PlaywrightDriver .
pg:SyncPlaywright a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Sync playwright"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's first Playwright program is from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright, a with block, playwright.firefox.launch(), browser.new_page(), page.goto(url), print(page.title()) and browser.close(); the with block calls start() and stop() for the program, and the synchronous mode means a function does not return until its operation is complete, so the program cannot ask for an element before the page has loaded (Sweigart, 2025). The asynchronous features are beyond the book's scope."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_SyncPlaywright> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SyncPlaywright" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PlaywrightDriver .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SyncPlaywright .
pg:HeadlessMode a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Headless mode"@en ;
    skos:definition "Playwright runs a browser in headless mode by default, without a window, which the chapter says makes it useful for automated tests and scraping in the background; to watch, the interactive shell form starts the browser with launch(headless=False, slow_mo=50), which shows the window and adds a 50 ms delay to each operation, and ends with browser.close() and playwright.stop() (Sweigart, 2025). The browsers themselves are installed with python -m playwright install, written in the chapter for macOS and Linux as python3 –m with an en dash."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_HeadlessMode> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-HeadlessMode" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PlaywrightDriver .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:HeadlessMode .
pg:PlaywrightButtons a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Playwright buttons"@en ;
    skos:definition "page.go_back() clicks the Back button, go_forward() the Forward button, reload() the Refresh button and close() the Close Window button, as Page methods on the object that browser.new_page() returns; a Page is a new tab in a new browser window, and several can be open at once (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_PlaywrightButtons> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PlaywrightButtons" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PlaywrightDriver .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PlaywrightButtons .
pg:PlaywrightElements a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Playwright elements"@en ;
    skos:definition "Playwright finds an element with a locator, a method of the Page that returns a Locator object standing for the elements that may match, and acts on it with the methods of Table 13-6 (Sweigart, 2025). A locator is a standing order for 'the element that looks like this', placed with the shop but filled when the page is ready."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_PlaywrightElements> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PlaywrightElements" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Browsers .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PlaywrightElements .
pg:PageLocators a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Page locators"@en ;
    skos:definition "Table 13-5 lists get_by_role, get_by_text, get_by_label, get_by_placeholder, get_by_alt_text and locator; the chapter explains roles as the ARIA standards that let software identify content for users with vision or other disabilities, such as the heading role for h1 to h6, and locates the link with get_by_text('is a link'), the checkbox with get_by_label('Agree'), the field with get_by_placeholder('admin') and the picture with get_by_alt_text('Zophie') (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_PageLocators> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PageLocators" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PlaywrightElements .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PageLocators .
pg:LocatorMethods a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Locator methods"@en ;
    skos:definition "Table 13-6 lists get_attribute, count, nth, first, last, all, inner_text, inner_html, click, is_visible, is_enabled, is_checked and bounding_box, and the chapter's program picks the first paragraph of the page with page.locator('p') and nth(0) (Sweigart, 2025). A Locator may stand for many elements, and nth gives it one."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_LocatorMethods> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LocatorMethods" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PlaywrightElements .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LocatorMethods .
pg:AutoWaiting a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Auto waiting"@en ;
    skos:definition "Because Playwright understands that a page might create an element later, it pauses for 30 seconds while it waits for an element that does not exist to appear, and the chapter gives is_visible() on a Locator and page.query_selector() as the checks that answer at once, query_selector returning None when the element is not there (Sweigart, 2025). Selenium, by contrast, raises an error at once."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_AutoWaiting> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AutoWaiting" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PlaywrightElements .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AutoWaiting .
pg:CheckBoxes a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Check boxes"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Page methods click(), check(), uncheck() and set_checked() simulate clicks on links, buttons and checkboxes; click toggles a checkbox to the opposite state, whereas check and uncheck leave it checked or unchecked whatever it was before, and set_checked takes True or False, so the chapter calls check and uncheck more reliable (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_CheckBoxes> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CheckBoxes" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PlaywrightElements .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CheckBoxes .
pg:FillAndPress a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Fill and press"@en ;
    skos:definition "A Locator's fill() types a string into an input or textarea, clear() erases it, and press() sends a key to the element, either a single character, a modifier such as Control+A, or a name such as End, Home, ArrowUp or Enter; unlike Selenium, there is no submit() and the Submit button is clicked (Sweigart, 2025). The chapter fills the example page's fake login."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_FillAndPress> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FillAndPress" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PlaywrightElements .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FillAndPress .
pg:ToolChoice a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Tool choice"@en ;
    skos:definition "The question under the whole chapter is which tool the page in front of the program needs: a browser launcher, a downloader and parser, or a driven browser (Sweigart, 2025). The chapter gives its answer in the first lines of its Selenium section and again in the Summary."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_ToolChoice> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ToolChoice" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Browsers .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ToolChoice .
pg:DynamicPages a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Dynamic pages"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that a page that depends on JavaScript that updates it, or that needs a login, calls for Selenium and not requests, that large sites recognise scripts and may refuse them, that a driven browser is more likely to work on those sites but slower and hard to run in the background, and that an API is the better choice when the site offers one (Sweigart, 2025). The tool is chosen by where the data is built."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_DynamicPages> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DynamicPages" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ToolChoice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DynamicPages .
pg:AutomationTells a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Automation tells"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that a major tell to websites that you are using a script is the user-agent string, that a Selenium-driven browser has the same user agent as a regular browser, and that it downloads images, advertisements, cookies and trackers like a regular browser, but that websites can still find ways to detect Selenium and major ticketing and e-commerce sites often block it (Sweigart, 2025). A driven browser is a person in a costume, and the costume has seams."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch13#S_AutomationTells> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AutomationTells" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ToolChoice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AutomationTells .
